      culturalHistory: "Uttarakhand's culture grew in two hill regions, Garhwal and Kumaon, each with its own language, foodways and deity circuits, and both organised around a calendar of sowing, harvest and pilgrimage. The twelve-yearly Nanda Devi Raj Jat, carrying the goddess from Nauti towards Kailash, is the grandest expression of that circuit and gives the state a Himalayan counterpart to the plains' great melas. In the high valleys the Bhotiya sustained trans-Himalayan trade until the mid-twentieth century, while the Terai forests held the Tharu and Buksa. In 2010 the state became the first in India to make Sanskrit a second official language.",
