      culturalHistory: "Bengal's public culture crystallised around the autumn Durga Puja, whose community (barowari) pandals turned a household rite into a mass civic art form; UNESCO's 2021 inscription cited exactly that combination of craft, inclusion and public creativity. The state's demography is unusually plural — a Bengali Hindu majority, India's second-largest Muslim share, a large Rajbanshi population in the north, a Nepali-speaking Darjeeling, and 40 Scheduled Tribes in the western laterite country — and language policy has followed, with the Official Language Act amended in 2012 and 2018 to give official use to ten further languages. Bengal's sweet-making tradition became a matter of formal law when \"Banglar Rasogolla\" received a geographical indication in 2017.",
